        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor(qa): add canonical live channel adapters",
        "body": "Closes #99699\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nTelegram, Slack, WhatsApp, and Matrix live QA transports could not be selected by the shared QA Lab scenario host. Matrix also belongs to its own QA runner plugin, so QA Lab needs an owner-neutral contribution contract rather than importing plugin-private runtime code.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nEach live channel contributes a transport adapter definition through the existing QA runner registration seam. QA Lab remains the single scenario host: it owns the QA bus, wait lifecycle, failure detection, and state-backed adapter wrapper, while channel factories own only transport setup, inbound delivery, outbound observation, readiness, cleanup, and channel configuration.\n\nThe runner contract now exposes only four adapter setup options and a three-method message recorder instead of duplicating the full CLI option object and QA bus state. Telegram Bot API types come from the Telegram plugin API, and Slack message types are derived from the existing validated response schema.\n\nThe existing `qa telegram`, `qa slack`, `qa whatsapp`, and `qa matrix` commands keep their current runners and scenario catalogs. The shared host can run migrated scenarios with `qa suite --channel-driver live --channel <adapter-id>` without changing current command behavior. Matrix cleanup and forced-exit behavior remain intact.\n\nQA Channel and Crabline keep their existing stateful implementations; contributed live definitions are the only transports wrapped by the shared host. Slack streamed previews are reconciled into QA bus edit events when the native message is updated in place.\n\n## User Impact\n\nExisting live QA commands behave as before. Maintainers can migrate shared YAML/flow scenarios to the canonical adapters incrementally, then remove legacy scenario runners only after their command contracts are represented by the shared host.\n\nExplicit shared live-adapter runs use one worker, reject runtime-pair duplication, and default to the selected factory's declared canonical scenarios.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Rebased onto `bfffa950d71c91b5a8f9c2f3bbd2d3a80da7705c`.\n- Focused SDK, QA host, registry, Crabline, CLI, Telegram, Slack, and boundary tests passed.\n- Extension test types passed, including the new Slack preview-to-final edit regression.\n- `pnpm build` passed with Plugin SDK export verification.\n- Extension package-boundary validation passed for all 117 plugins plus canaries; Telegram now uses the same generated public-API declaration boundary as Slack and WhatsApp.\n- `git diff --check` passed.\n- Final Codex autoreview is clean with no accepted/actionable findings.\n- Exact-content Testbox-through-Crabbox `tbx_01kwqtr4x789kqdgeh9azfydfq` passed for head `ddc617494c240e7beeef16577e5b1df73b1cf559` (Actions run 28724355393). Current-head CI has 68 passing substantive checks with Real behavior proof still in progress. Current-head ClawSweeper is pinned to this SHA with no contributor-facing code finding.\n",
